# Every '@org/...' handle here is a PLACEHOLDER. '@org' is not a real GitHub
# organisation, and GitHub silently ignores an owner it cannot resolve, so as
# shipped this file requires review from nobody. Point ownership.yaml at your
# organisation and replace every handle below before relying on it.

# Default owner for anything not matched below.
* @org/platform

# Paths that govern the checks themselves. A team must not be able to approve
# changes to the contract that governs its own changes, so these stay with the
# platform team regardless of who is editing rules.
#
# Order matters: GitHub applies the LAST pattern that matches a path, so any
# entry added below these can take a governed file back out of platform hands.
# scripts/rulecheck.py checks exactly that and fails the build if it happens.
/Chart.yaml @org/platform
/values.yaml @org/platform
/values.schema.json @org/platform
/templates/ @org/platform
/validation.yaml @org/platform
/scripts/ @org/platform
/tests/ @org/platform
/tools/ @org/platform
/Makefile @org/platform
/requirements.txt @org/platform
/.github/ @org/platform
# ownership.yaml names the organisation every other entry is checked against, so
# a team able to edit it could redirect ownership of everything.
/ownership.yaml @org/platform

# Per-team ownership. A team owns its own rules AND its own dashboards, and it
# owns them alone: on GitHub any co-owner can approve by itself. Both entries
# are required for every team, and rulecheck.py fails the build without them.
/rules/platform/ @org/platform
/dashboards/platform/ @org/platform
